Oxidation states of the metals:
(a) $\text{CrO}_3$: $+6$;
(d) $\text{V}_2\text{O}_5$: $+5$;
(c) $\text{MnO}_2$: $+4$;
(b) $\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3$: $+3$;
(e) $\text{Cu}_2\text{O}$: $+1$.
Therefore, the decreasing order of oxidation numbers is $(a) > (d) > (c) > (b) > (e)$.

$\mu = \sqrt{n(n+2)}\text{ BM}$:
$\text{Ti}^{3+} = [\text{Ar}]3d^1$ ($n = 1 \implies \mu = \sqrt{3} = 1.73\text{ BM}$);
$\text{V}^{2+} = [\text{Ar}]3d^3$ ($n = 3 \implies \mu = \sqrt{15} = 3.87\text{ BM}$);
$\text{Sc}^{3+} = [\text{Ar}]3d^0 4s^0$ ($n = 0 \implies \mu = 0\text{ BM}$).

(A) $\text{Cu}^{2+}$ ($3d^9$) has $1$ unpaired electron, so mononuclear $\text{Cu}(\text{II})$ complexes are paramagnetic (Correct);
(B) $\text{Cu}^+$ ($3d^{10}$) has completely filled $d$-orbitals and no $d-d$ transitions, so its complexes are generally colourless (Correct);
(C) $\text{Cu}(\text{I})$ readily undergoes oxidation/disproportionation to $\text{Cu}(\text{II})$ in aqueous medium (Correct);
(D) Fehling's solution contains alkaline $\text{Cu}^{2+}$ complexed with tartrate ions, not $\text{Cu}(\text{I})$ (Incorrect).
Thus, $3$ statements are correct.

