$2\text{Na}_2\text{CrO}_4(\text{A}) + 2\text{H}^+ \longrightarrow \text{Na}_2\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7(\text{B}) + 2\text{Na}^+ + \text{H}_2\text{O}$.
In $\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-}$, there is $1$ bridging oxygen ($\text{Cr}-\text{O}-\text{Cr}$) and $6$ terminal oxygen atoms.

Number of unpaired electrons ($n$):
$\text{V}^{3+}: [\text{Ar}]3d^2 \implies n = 2$;
$\text{Cr}^{3+}: [\text{Ar}]3d^3 \implies n = 3$;
$\text{Fe}^{2+}: [\text{Ar}]3d^6 \implies n = 4$;
$\text{Ni}^{3+}: [\text{Ar}]3d^7 \implies n = 3$.
Thus, $2$ ions ($\text{Cr}^{3+}$ and $\text{Ni}^{3+}$) have the same number of unpaired electrons ($n = 3$) and identical spin-only magnetic moment ($3.87\text{ BM}$).

(i) $\text{Mn}$ exhibits $+7$ in $\text{Mn}_2\text{O}_7$ (Correct);
(ii) $\text{Ru}$ and $\text{Os}$ exhibit $+8$ in $\text{RuO}_4$ and $\text{OsO}_4$ (Correct);
(iii) $\text{Sc}$ has only $3$ valence electrons ($3d^1 4s^2$) and never exhibits $+4$ oxidation state (Incorrect);
(iv) $\text{Cr}(\text{VI})$ (e.g., $\text{Cr}_2\text{O}_7^{2-}$) is a strong oxidising agent (Correct).
Thus, (i), (ii) and (iv) are correct.

